course: medicine
lesson: 18
title: The right dose and never more than
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Every medicine has a right dose: too little does nothing, too much can harm, and "never more than four doses in 24 hours" on the box is a real safety line.
explain: Too much paracetamol can seriously damage the liver even if you feel fine at first, so grown-ups write down the time of each dose.
explain: Your dose depends on your age and weight, which is why yours differs from your little sister's, and why someone else's tablets are never safe for you.
explain: If you forget a dose, never double up; the leaflet or pharmacist says what to do.
explain: Shop medicines and vitamins are not sweets: swallowing too many is an emergency, so a grown-up calls 111 or 999 and brings the packet.
fact: Too much paracetamol can seriously damage the liver, even if you feel fine at first.
fact: Parents write down the time of each dose and check that no other medicine they give also contains paracetamol.
fact: Children's doses depend on age and weight, because a bigger body needs more medicine for the same effect.
fact: Medicine prescribed for one person is chosen for their illness, age, weight and other medicines, so never take someone else's.
fact: Some vitamins and iron tablets can be dangerous in large amounts, especially to small children.
fact: If a child swallows pills by mistake, a grown-up calls NHS 111, the health advice phone line, or a poisons service, or 999 if the child is unwell, and brings the packet; making yourself sick is not the right thing.
fact: Liquid medicines come in different strengths, so the strength is checked and the line read at eye level, because looking from above or below makes you misread it.
fact: Doctors usually suggest one of paracetamol or ibuprofen first, and switching to the other only if the first hasn't helped.
fact: A pill organiser with the days of the week helps people who take several medicines take the right ones at the right time, and a pharmacist can check whether someone still needs all of them.
fact: After a missed dose you usually take it when you remember, unless it's nearly time for the next one, and you never take two to catch up.
fact: Out-of-date medicines can lose strength or change; pharmacies take old medicines back, and flushing them harms rivers and wildlife.
fact: Even shop medicines such as cough medicine can be dangerous in big amounts, and any overdose (too much medicine) is an emergency.
exercise: Why does the box say never more than four doses in 24 hours? | Too much can seriously harm, for paracetamol the liver | So the box doesn't run out before the week is over and you need more | Medicine tastes worse after four | It's just a suggestion from the makers
exercise: Why is your dose different from your little sister's? | Doses depend on age and weight | Older children are braver about medicine | Girls always need less medicine than boys | The pharmacist just guessed by looking
exercise: Your friend offers you his tablets for your headache. What should you do? | Say no; never take someone else's medicine | Take one, since it helped him with his | Take two at once to make it work much quicker | Take half of one
exercise: You swallowed your sister's vitamins by mistake. What should a grown-up do? | Call 111 or a poisons service and bring the packet | Make you sick straight away with a glass of salty water | Nothing, vitamins are harmless | Give you more to balance it
exercise: Why does Dad check the line on the syringe twice? | A small mistake means a wrong dose | The syringe changes size when it's warm | It's just a lucky habit he picked up | The medicine moves around inside the tube
exercise: Why shouldn't paracetamol and ibuprofen usually be given together just to bring a fever down? | Doctors suggest one first and switching only if it hasn't helped | They cancel each other out in the tummy | They fizz and explode together | Both together cure everything at once, so it's a waste of one
exercise: What is grandma's box with days of the week for? | Taking the right pills at the right time | Storing a sweet for each day of the week | Counting down to her birthday | Keeping her spare buttons tidy
exercise: You forgot this morning's medicine. What should you never do? | Take two doses to catch up | Ask a grown-up what to do | Read the leaflet in the box | Take it when you remember, if the leaflet says so
exercise: What should you do with out-of-date medicine? | Take it back to a pharmacy | Flush it down the toilet with water | Take it anyway, it still works | Throw it in the river or a pond
exercise: Why are shop medicines dangerous in big amounts? | Any medicine can harm in an overdose | They can't be, they're sold in an ordinary shop | Only prescription medicines can harm | Only if they're old and past their date
exercise: Why must parents check other medicines for paracetamol? | Two medicines could both contain it, doubling the dose | Paracetamol is rare and hard to find | To find the cheapest packet | Paracetamol stops all the other medicines from working properly
exercise: Why can feeling fine after too much paracetamol be misleading? | The liver can be damaged before you feel ill | It means no harm was done at all to your body | It means you're immune to it now | It means the medicine was fake
exercise: Is half an adult tablet always right for a child? | No, the child's dose must be checked for age or weight | Yes, always, that's exactly what all children's doses are | Yes, if it's cut neatly | No, a whole one is better
exercise: Why does flushing medicines matter? | It harms rivers and wildlife | It only blocks the toilet pipes up | It makes tap water taste sweet | It doesn't matter at all where it goes
exercise: Your cousin took cough medicine for fun and got very ill. How should an overdose be treated? | As an emergency | As a silly joke, nothing more | With a bit more cough medicine | With a long nap
test: headache's not gone after 1 dose, chuck another in early? | No, wait the time on the packet; extra is not safe | Yeah, a double dose helps it kick in faster | Yeah, take three | No, take your friend's pills instead
test: Your little sister's medicine is 5 ml and yours is 10 ml. Is one a mistake? | Not necessarily; doses depend on age and weight | Yes, all kids take exactly the same dose, whatever their size | Yes, sisters always take more | No, but swap them anyway to be fair
test: In a cartoon, a boy eats a whole bottle of gummy vitamins and grows huge muscles. What's true? | Too many vitamins can make you ill; it's an emergency | Vitamins really make you super strong if you eat enough | More vitamins are always better | Gummy vitamins are just sweets
test: Mum gave me cold syrup and then paracetamol. What should she check first? | Whether the syrup already has paracetamol in it | Whether the syrup is the red or the yellow kind | Whether I'm hungry | Nothing, mixing any medicines together is fine
test: The cough mixture is a year out of date. Dad says it's fine. What's the advice? | Return it to a pharmacy; old medicine can lose strength or change | Drink it all quickly before it gets worse | Pour it down the sink | Give it to the dog
test: Lily swallowed some pills and Mum wants to make her sick. What's right? | Don't make her sick; call 111 or 999 and bring the packet | Yes, make her sick | Give her a glass of milk and wait a few hours | Give her more pills
test: What would happen if Grandma took her Monday pills twice by mistake? | She could get too much medicine, so call a pharmacist or 111 | Nothing, more pills are healthier | She'd just skip Tuesday's pills | Her two lots of pills would cancel each other out in her blood
test: Why does Dad read the syringe line at eye level? | Looking from above or below can make you misread it | It looks nicer | The line moves up and down if you look down at it | It's an old family habit his own dad taught him
test: My friend says it's fine to share his antibiotics because he got better. Right? | No, his medicine was chosen for him | Yes, it worked for him, so it'll work for you | Yes, half each, so you both get some | No, only ever share painkillers, not those
test: Forgot my morning medicine and the evening dose is in an hour. What should I do? | Check the leaflet or ask; usually don't take two close together | Take both now | Take three tonight | Throw the rest of the medicine away and start again tomorrow
test: Grandma takes eight pills a day. More pills means healthier? | No, a pharmacist can review whether she needs them all | Yes, more is better | Yes, double them | No, she should stop them all herself and see how she feels
exam: Identify the principal hazard associated with an excessive dose of paracetamol. | Serious damage to the liver, which may initially be without symptoms | An immediate and harmless drowsiness, passing within the hour | An improvement in the efficacy of the medicine | No hazard whatsoever
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the determination of a child's dose? | The dose is determined by the child's age and weight | The dose is identical for all children | The dose is determined by the child's preference | Half an adult tablet is invariably appropriate
exam: It is given that a dose of medicine has been omitted. Select the response consistent with established guidance. | Two doses are never to be taken together to compensate | The omitted dose is to be doubled at the next interval | The entire course is to be abandoned | Three doses are to be taken at bedtime
exam: State the recommended disposal of medicines whose date of expiry has passed. | Return to a pharmacy for safe disposal | Disposal by flushing into the public sewer | Consumption without delay | Disposal into a watercourse
